That's why I was trying to get your timeline really, of your curiosity, you went away before Elliot was born on that first trip to South America, and then your second one was when Elliot was a few months old, which is how I remember it.
这就是为什么我真的想弄清楚你的时间线，关于你的好奇心，你在第一次去南美旅行时，是在Elliot出生之前，然后你的第二次旅行是在Elliot几个月大的时候，我是这么记得的。

00:37:45.739 --> 00:37:46.780
What was his birthday?
他的生日是什么时候？

00:37:46.780 --> 00:37:52.798
His birthday is April 30th, so he was born on April 30th of 2007, and so that would have been, yeah, right?
他的生日是4月30日，所以他是2007年4月30日出生的，所以那应该是……是的，对吧？

00:37:52.798 --> 00:37:57.579
And I think we would have been on hiatus for over a month or something at that point.
我想那时我们已经停拍了一个多月了。

00:37:57.579 --> 00:38:02.968
And it was like, oh, perfect timing, hiatus, baby, he'll be born, it'll be the, well, I'll have the baby.
当时觉得，哦，时机太完美了，停拍，生孩子，他会出生，我会照顾孩子。

00:38:02.968 --> 00:38:05.739
I was really going to be the first in my friend group.
我真的会成为我朋友圈里第一个生孩子的。

00:38:05.739 --> 00:38:12.059
One of them, Alec was, Alec, our producer was also having a baby in that same hiatus.
其中一个，Alec，我们的制片人，也在同一个间歇期有了孩子。

00:38:12.059 --> 00:38:18.780
But we were young, not everyone had kids on the show, the cast, we were like, ah, we're going first.
但我们当时很年轻，剧组里并不是每个人都有孩子，我们当时想，啊，我们要先来。

00:38:18.780 --> 00:38:19.780
And it would be great.
那会很棒。

00:38:19.780 --> 00:38:27.940
The baby will come out, it will be a nice couple months with the baby and then back to work, easy, peasy, and then life will all make sense and have a perfect shape after that.
孩子出生后，我们会和孩子一起度过美好的几个月，然后回到工作岗位，轻而易举，之后生活就会变得顺理成章，有一个完美的形状。

00:38:27.940 --> 00:38:29.340
That's not what happened.
但事实并非如此。

00:38:29.340 --> 00:38:38.051
What happened is that Elliot was born and we had no idea until he was born that he needed open heart surgery, he weighed much less than anticipated.
发生的事情是，Elliot出生了，在他出生之前我们根本不知道他需要进行心脏直视手术，他的体重比预期的要轻得多。

00:38:38.051 --> 00:38:45.350
No wonder what it was for the first week, the thought was he has viral pneumonia, he has this, he has that it was all wrong.
难怪第一周的时候，大家以为他患了病毒性肺炎，或者这个那个，全都不对。

00:38:45.350 --> 00:38:58.299
We had a bunch of misdiagnoses at one hospital, we moved to this hospital children's hospital of Los Angeles, which was amazing and saved my son's life, diagnosed him correctly and saved his life with open heart surgery.
我们在一家医院经历了一连串的误诊，然后转到了洛杉矶儿童医院，那里的医生太棒了，他们挽救了我儿子的生命，正确诊断了他的病情，并通过心脏直视手术救了他。

00:38:58.299 --> 00:39:09.247
And what he had was something called Jacobson syndrome, which is a genetic syndrome where, for no reason at all, just a random chance, it's called the de novo deletion, a lightning bolt.
他患的是一种叫做雅各布森综合征的病，这是一种遗传综合征，没有任何原因，纯属偶然，这被称为“从头缺失”，就像一道闪电。

00:39:09.247 --> 00:39:17.900
A little piece of the 11th chromosome is missing, which means there are a bunch of health ramifications and a bunch of developmental ramifications.
第11号染色体缺失了一小块，这意味着会产生一系列健康和发育方面的后果。

00:39:18.940 --> 00:39:19.633
So he was born.
所以他出生了。

00:39:19.633 --> 00:39:21.530
We were called down to the bad news room.
我们被叫到了坏消息室。

00:39:21.530 --> 00:39:28.054
We had been in the neonatal intensive care unit with this kid who weighed three and a half pounds, who were told he needs open heart surgery.
我们一直在新生儿重症监护室陪着这个体重只有三磅半的孩子，被告知他需要进行心脏直视手术。

00:39:28.054 --> 00:39:30.459
That's what he was born at, three and a half pounds.
他出生时就是那个体重，三磅半。

00:39:30.460 --> 00:39:33.460
It was like three pounds, 10 out of 11 ounces, something like that.
大概是三磅十到十一盎司左右。

00:39:33.460 --> 00:39:36.460
And they couldn't tell that from like the scan.
他们从扫描中看不出来吗？

00:39:36.460 --> 00:39:38.820
They thought he was small, my wife is five feet tall.
他们以为他很小，我妻子身高五英尺。

00:39:38.820 --> 00:39:41.500
They were like, she was a small baby, but they were off.
他们说，她是个小婴儿，但他们估计错了。

00:39:41.500 --> 00:39:43.460
The estimation, it's so funny.
那个估算，太搞笑了。

00:39:43.460 --> 00:39:44.780
2007 is long enough.
2007年已经过去很久了。

00:39:44.780 --> 00:39:47.179
I got that like technology has gotten much better.
我知道现在的技术已经好多了。

00:39:47.179 --> 00:39:48.980
The scans would have shown more.
扫描会显示更多信息。

00:39:48.980 --> 00:39:50.619
Things would have been more accurate.
情况会更准确。

00:39:50.619 --> 00:39:53.340
They thought he was always a little small, but I guess it makes sense.
他们一直以为他有点小，但我想这说得通。

00:39:53.340 --> 00:39:57.820
The mom's small, but it turned out to be smaller than they thought.
妈妈个子小，但结果比他们想的还要小。

00:39:57.820 --> 00:40:04.444
And he had the open heart surgery, and all the doctors thought was he was born with these random heart problems.
他做了心脏直视手术，所有的医生都以为他只是天生有这些随机的心脏问题。

00:40:04.444 --> 00:40:05.450
No one knows why.
没人知道为什么。

00:40:05.450 --> 00:40:15.564
And only after the successful life-saving open heart surgery where they were operating on this tiny heart with the description wasn't so vivid and vivid and unforgettable.
只有在成功挽救生命的心脏直视手术之后，当他们在那颗微小的心脏上进行手术时，那种描述是如此生动、如此令人难忘。

00:40:15.564 --> 00:40:20.059
And this is not what you want to be thinking about when you're a new parent.
当你是一个新手父母时，你可不想去想这些。

00:40:20.059 --> 00:40:24.219
They said, we'll be operating on veins the size of dental floss.
他们说，我们要在像牙线一样细的血管上进行手术。

00:40:24.219 --> 00:40:25.059
Oh, my God.
天哪。

00:40:25.059 --> 00:40:34.460
And you say, oh, and they tell you all the percentages of ways it could go wrong, and it adds up to like, this is a 50-50 search, but the outcome's 50-50.
然后他们告诉你所有可能出错的百分比，加起来就像是，这是一个五五开的手术，结果也是五五开。

00:40:34.460 --> 00:40:37.659
Toss a coin of whether he will live.
抛硬币决定他是否能活下来。

00:40:37.659 --> 00:40:46.300
And we, it was a successful open heart surgery to fixing four things, two holes in the wall of his heart.
我们，那是一次成功的心脏直视手术，修复了四个问题，包括他心脏壁上的两个洞。

00:40:46.300 --> 00:40:51.099
This extra piece called a core triadriatum, which is just this deformed part that had to be fixed.
这块多出来的部分叫三房心，只是这部分畸形必须得修复。

00:40:51.099 --> 00:40:53.980
But not one of the, that's the least of the problems.
但这还不是最严重的问题，这只是最轻微的问题之一。

00:40:53.980 --> 00:40:56.579
and the two holes are closeable on ASD and a VSD.
房间隔缺损（ASD）和室间隔缺损（VSD）这两个孔是可以闭合的。

00:40:56.579 --> 00:41:11.420
And then this much bigger problem called total anomalous pulmonary venous return TAPVR that is the really wrongly wired veins that went instead of coming from the heart back to the lungs went down to the liver.
还有一个更大的问题叫完全性肺静脉异位引流（TAPVR），就是静脉接错了，本该从心脏回到肺部的静脉却流向了肝脏。

00:41:11.420 --> 00:41:24.639
And did this incredible, what in retrospect was this incredible improvisation in order, what was really going on is the genetic map to properly form a heart was off because of the missing piece of that chromosome.
当时医生做了一次不可思议的、事后看来简直是神来之笔的临时手术，因为真正的问题是由于染色体缺失，导致心脏形成的基因图谱出了错。

00:41:24.639 --> 00:41:43.679
But Elliot, improvisational musician that he is today at age 19, his heart improvised a route and the veins were sent somewhere else and like MacGyverd their way back throw through the liver into his heart to exist in utero.
但埃利奥特，就像他今天19岁时展现出的即兴音乐天赋一样，他的心脏也“即兴”出了一条路径，静脉被送到了别处，就像用“百战天龙”式的办法，穿过肝脏回到心脏，从而在子宫内存活了下来。

00:41:43.679 --> 00:41:47.800
But once you are born, you cannot get enough oxygen that way.
但一旦出生，这种方式就无法提供足够的氧气了。

00:41:47.800 --> 00:41:49.880
And that's why his oxygen was so low.
这就是他血氧含量那么低的原因。

00:41:49.880 --> 00:41:51.960
but no one knew why.
但没人知道为什么。

00:41:51.960 --> 00:42:02.159
And they had to fix all of these four heart problems in a half hour by stopping his heart and pumping his blood through a machine.
他们必须在半小时内修复这四个心脏问题，方法是让他的心脏停跳，并用机器泵送血液。

00:42:02.159 --> 00:42:06.519
And if it's any longer than a half hour, it's the disaster.
如果超过半小时，那就是一场灾难。

00:42:06.519 --> 00:42:17.519
So this amazing doctor, Dr. von Starns at Children's Hospital of Los Angeles, who many years later is the doctor who did the open heart surgery on Jimmy Kimmel's son.
所以这位了不起的医生，洛杉矶儿童医院的冯·斯塔恩斯医生，多年后他也是为吉米·坎摩尔的儿子做开胸手术的那位医生。

00:42:17.519 --> 00:42:24.199
And also, and this is, he's usually a specialist, people fly from all over the world to see this doctor, Dr. Starnes.
而且，他通常是一位专家，人们从世界各地飞来找这位斯塔恩斯医生看病。

00:42:24.199 --> 00:42:32.559
Usually he does only kids, or mainly kids, but he also operated on Arnold Schwarzenegger and did a quintuple bypass, I think.
他通常只看孩子，或者主要是看孩子，但他还给阿诺德·施瓦辛格做过手术，我想是做了五重搭桥手术。

00:42:32.559 --> 00:42:38.579
So I'd like to say Elliot and the Terminator's heart had the same dude's hands on it on them.
所以我喜欢说，埃利奥特和终结者的心脏都经由同一个人的手治疗过。

00:42:39.480 --> 00:42:40.880
And Jimmy Kimmel's son.
还有吉米·坎摩尔的儿子。

00:42:40.880 --> 00:42:43.880
So we all have a weird little bond.
所以我们之间有一种奇妙的联系。

00:42:43.880 --> 00:42:50.960
isn't it weird that in one summer, you became like a pretty quick cardiac expert.
在一个夏天里，你变得像个心脏病专家一样，这不是很奇怪吗？

00:42:50.960 --> 00:42:53.440
Like you still know these terms today.
你到现在还记得这些术语。

00:42:53.440 --> 00:42:55.239
Oh yeah, my wife is the genius.
哦，是的，我妻子才是天才。

00:42:55.239 --> 00:43:02.480
My wife has retained all of this and still does and there's still things we have to know about the heart and she is the genius library about.
我妻子记住了所有这些，现在依然记得，关于心脏我们还有很多需要了解的地方，她是这方面的天才图书馆。

00:43:02.480 --> 00:43:08.519
She discovered this facility and for this language and I'm okay at it, but she's amazing at it.
她发现了这个机构和这套术语，我只是还行，但她非常厉害。

00:43:08.519 --> 00:43:14.239
She had to just become an expert because Jacobson syndrome is very, very rare.
她不得不成为专家，因为雅各布森综合征非常、非常罕见。

00:43:14.239 --> 00:43:25.079
So we wound up, my hiatus between season two and three was six and a half weeks in the neonatal intensive care unit with my son in a plastic box.
所以最终，我在第二季和第三季之间的空档期，是在新生儿重症监护室里度过的，我的儿子被关在一个塑料盒子里，待了六个半星期。

00:43:25.079 --> 00:43:28.599
And it was so strange.
那感觉太奇怪了。

00:43:28.599 --> 00:43:33.119
Our lives just felt like they had gone to in a completely different direction than anticipated.
我们的生活感觉完全偏离了预期的方向。

00:43:33.119 --> 00:43:39.119
And in the back of my head is each day ticked away, I thought I would have to think, I've got a launch season three of this TV show.
每天过去的时候，我脑子里都在想，我得考虑我得启动这部电视剧的第三季。

00:43:39.119 --> 00:43:39.960
Pretty soon.
很快就要开始了。

00:43:39.960 --> 00:43:40.800
Now go be funny.
现在去搞笑吧。

00:43:40.800 --> 00:43:41.300
be funny.
去搞笑。

00:43:41.300 --> 00:43:42.537
That's what I would say.
我当时就是这么对自己说的。

00:43:42.537 --> 00:43:47.024
I would go into the rants and to run running the show in the beginning of season three.
在第三季开始时，我会去进行咆哮，去负责运营节目。

00:43:47.024 --> 00:43:52.800
And I would plop down the couch in my office with Carter and we'd look into each other and say, now go be funny.
我会和卡特一起瘫倒在办公室的沙发上，我们看着对方说，现在去搞笑吧。

00:43:52.800 --> 00:43:57.339
It was like I was living in this intense medical drama and then commuting into a sitcom.
感觉就像我生活在一部紧张的医疗剧中，然后又去通勤演情景喜剧。

00:43:57.339 --> 00:43:59.195
It was just such a bizarre whiplash.
那种反差感真是太离奇了。

00:43:59.159 --> 00:44:01.771
Can I ask you a couple more questions?
我能再问你几个问题吗？

00:44:01.771 --> 00:44:03.971
How long was he in the hospital?
他在医院住了多久？

00:44:03.971 --> 00:44:05.552
It was about six weeks.
大约六个星期。

00:44:05.552 --> 00:44:08.301
So were you guys living at the hospital?
那你们当时住在医院里吗？

00:44:08.301 --> 00:44:08.920
Were you?
是吗？

00:44:08.920 --> 00:44:30.039
We had one point where I think staying in a hotel room at a hospital, but we didn't live that far from it, so we eventually were spending a bunch of time right at the hospital and almost never leaving and just going to a hotel to crash for a few hours and going to the hospital, but eventually we realized we were dying inside and we needed to start going home and shifting it out a little bit.
有一次我想我们住在医院的酒店房间里，但我们住的地方离医院不远，所以我们最终大部分时间都待在医院，几乎从不离开，只是去酒店睡几个小时，然后又回医院，但最终我们意识到我们快崩溃了，我们需要开始回家，稍微调整一下。

00:44:30.039 --> 00:44:31.639
Were you guys able to hold him?
你们能抱他吗？

00:44:31.639 --> 00:44:32.639
No.
不能。

00:44:32.639 --> 00:44:43.142
We were mostly not able to hold him for a while, so after that surgery he was hooked up to so many tubes, he had this, you know, gnarly scar down his chest held together by staples and metal and stitches.
有一段时间我们大多不能抱他，手术后他身上插满了管子，你知道，他胸口有一道狰狞的伤疤，用钉子、金属和缝线固定着。

00:44:43.142 --> 00:45:01.624
At one point, the gnarliest moment was, and I'll just talk about this because who, maybe someone out there needs to hear this, maybe someone's been through this, maybe someone will hear this and later go through this, but they can't close up the chest due to swelling after an open heart surgery when the baby weighs three pounds, so they had to tape it shut.
有一次，最可怕的时刻是——我之所以说出来，是因为也许有人需要听到这些，也许有人经历过这些，也许有人以后会经历这些——由于开胸手术后肿胀，当婴儿只有三磅重时，他们无法缝合胸腔，所以他们不得不把它用胶带封住。

00:45:01.624 --> 00:45:04.301
So you could see the heart beating through the tape.
所以你可以透过胶带看到心脏在跳动。

00:45:04.301 --> 00:45:07.802
And my wife made the terrible mistake, tough, tough guy that she is.
我妻子犯了一个可怕的错误，尽管她是个坚强的女人。

00:45:07.802 --> 00:45:10.273
She's like, I'm going to go take a look at that.
她说，我要去看看那个。

00:45:10.273 --> 00:45:12.178
They really recommended not doing it.
他们真的建议不要这样做。

00:45:12.178 --> 00:45:14.391
Like, we're going to leave this covered up.
就像是，我们还是把它盖住吧。

00:45:14.391 --> 00:45:16.862
It's only going to be this way for a day or two.
只会这样维持一两天。

00:45:16.862 --> 00:45:21.187
My wife is like, she's like this dancer, choreographer, kinesiologist, anatomy nerd.
我妻子是个舞者、编舞家、运动机能学家，还是个解剖学书呆子。

00:45:21.187 --> 00:45:23.040
She's like, I think I can handle it.
她说，我想我能应付。

00:45:23.040 --> 00:45:24.430
I'm going to go look at it.
我要去看看。

00:45:24.430 --> 00:45:25.769
She went and looked at it.
她去看了。

00:45:25.769 --> 00:45:27.880
She was like, bring that chair over here.
她说，把那把椅子拿过来。

00:45:27.880 --> 00:45:31.154
I think I'm going to pass out and split a chair on her and she didn't.
我想我快晕倒了，我把椅子推给她，她没晕倒。

00:45:31.154 --> 00:45:33.400
She didn't, but that she couldn't stand anymore.
她没晕倒，但她已经站不住了。

00:45:33.400 --> 00:45:37.559
And so that, it was just the most visceral experience.
所以那真的是一种最发自肺腑的体验。

00:45:37.559 --> 00:45:43.360
And then of course, the geneticist calls you down, we celebrated that he lived through this open hearts injury.
然后当然，遗传学家把你叫下去，我们庆祝他挺过了这次开胸手术。

00:45:43.360 --> 00:45:47.000
And then the genetic, we get this message, the geneticist wants to see you now.
然后遗传学家，我们收到消息，遗传学家现在想见你。

00:45:47.000 --> 00:45:48.280
And we're like, okay, yeah, send her in here.
我们说，好的，让她进来吧。

00:45:48.280 --> 00:45:49.760
Here we are at the Isolette.
我们就在保温箱旁边。

00:45:49.760 --> 00:45:53.119
The word for those plastic boxes is called an Isolette.
那些塑料盒子的词叫“保温箱”（Isolette）。

00:45:53.119 --> 00:45:55.679
Isolette is the perfect fucking word.
Isolette这个词真是该死的贴切。

00:45:55.679 --> 00:46:07.639
Isolette, it sounds like some haunted fairy tale world, isolate, and you feel very alone when you're in and around the isolate, and we get called.
Isolette，听起来像是一个闹鬼的童话世界，隔离（isolate），当你身处保温箱周围时，你会感到非常孤独，然后我们被叫走了。

00:46:07.639 --> 00:46:09.517
We were like, oh, send her in here.
我们当时说，哦，让她进来吧。

00:46:09.517 --> 00:46:10.429
We live here now.
我们现在住在这里。

00:46:10.429 --> 00:46:12.199
We live next to this plastic box.
我们住在塑料盒子旁边。

00:46:12.199 --> 00:46:12.746
No, no.
不，不。

00:46:12.746 --> 00:46:17.198
The geneticist has booked a private room to speak to you.
遗传学家预订了一个私人房间来和你谈话。

00:46:17.198 --> 00:46:35.319
I'm like, that doesn't seem good, and you do that long walk down this hallway, you find the room, you open the door, it's a windowless room, couch, couple chairs, Kleenex, just the box of tissues, and like, this is the bad newsroom.
我想，这看起来不太妙，你沿着走廊走很长一段路，找到房间，打开门，那是一个没有窗户的房间，有沙发、几把椅子、面巾纸，就那一盒纸巾，就像是，这就是坏消息室。

00:46:35.320 --> 00:46:40.072
And that was the room where we were told that this isn't just hard problems.
那个房间就是我们被告知这不仅仅是心脏问题的地方。

00:46:40.072 --> 00:46:41.761
This is a genetic syndrome.
这是一种遗传综合征。

00:46:41.761 --> 00:46:43.575
He'll have it his whole life.
他终生都会患有这种病。

00:46:43.575 --> 00:46:55.957
The geneticist who had never seen an actual kid in her career with Jacobson syndrome, because it's that rare, basically had the Google print out of like everything associated with Jacobson syndrome.
那位遗传学家在她的职业生涯中从未见过真正的雅各布森综合征患儿，因为这太罕见了，她基本上拿着谷歌打印出来的所有与雅各布森综合征相关的信息。

00:46:55.957 --> 00:46:59.960
Jacobson syndrome has a wide spectrum, a wide range of outcomes.
雅各布森综合征有一个广泛的谱系，结果范围很广。

00:47:00.599 --> 00:47:10.984
There's a great diversity even within this rare community, but we just got read all the worst things kind of, you know, and with no tempering of like, but I've seen a few kids with Jacobson syndrome.
即使在这个罕见的群体中也有很大的多样性，但我们只是被读了所有最糟糕的事情，你知道，没有任何缓和，比如“但我见过几个患有雅各布森综合征的孩子”。

00:47:10.984 --> 00:47:15.733
And I've seen that there are great outcomes, which is there are, which there is, there are.
我看到过有很好的结果，确实有，确实有。

00:47:15.733 --> 00:47:17.194
And now Elliot's among them.
现在埃利奥特就是其中之一。

00:47:17.194 --> 00:47:19.021
Elliot is, Elliot is doing so well.
埃利奥特，埃利奥特做得非常好。

00:47:19.021 --> 00:47:24.240
He's not, And I probably should have started the story by saying this, but he's 19 in doing so well.
他不是……我可能应该在故事开头就说这一点，但他19岁了，做得非常好。

00:47:24.239 --> 00:47:32.380
He's doing things I never could have dreamt of when I was being read this litany of worst case scenarios in this bad newsroom.
他正在做的事情，我在那个坏消息室里听到这一连串最坏的情况时，做梦都想不到。

00:47:32.380 --> 00:47:38.760
And basically in this room, our hearts were just our hearts were opened up and sliced up.
基本上在那个房间里，我们的心被剖开并切碎了。

00:47:38.760 --> 00:47:45.719
And your hopes and dreams and what you thought, the whole way you thought life was going to go, no, that's all done.
你的希望、梦想和你认为生活会走的路，不，这一切都结束了。

00:47:45.719 --> 00:47:47.719
Here's every worst way it's going to go.
这就是它会发生的最糟糕的情况。

00:47:47.719 --> 00:47:51.059
of course your brain thinks that all of that will be true.
当然你的大脑会认为这一切都会成真。

00:47:51.059 --> 00:47:52.671
It's not, it's not all true.
事实并非如此，并非全部成真。

00:47:52.671 --> 00:47:53.708
Some of it's true.
有些是真的。

00:47:53.708 --> 00:47:54.687
Some of it's not.
有些不是。

00:47:54.687 --> 00:47:57.220
Some of it's gray areas and amounts of true.
有些是灰色地带，程度不一。

00:47:57.220 --> 00:47:59.120
He has a lot of extra challenges.
他有很多额外的挑战。

00:47:59.120 --> 00:48:00.560
He has health challenges.
他有健康挑战。

00:48:00.560 --> 00:48:02.402
He has developmental challenges.
他有发育挑战。

00:48:02.402 --> 00:48:05.224
He also has amazing strengths and amazing spirit.
他也有惊人的力量和惊人的精神。

00:48:05.224 --> 00:48:10.003
And what we said to ourselves was, well, we're going to see what this kid shows us.
我们对自己说的是，好吧，我们要看看这个孩子会向我们展示什么。

00:48:10.003 --> 00:48:14.840
Is this kid showing us that he wants to be on this planet and be part of this world?
这个孩子是在向我们展示他想留在这个星球上，成为这个世界的一部分吗？

00:48:14.840 --> 00:48:25.360
And if he does, if he survives this and he shows us that he wants to stay here, then we are going to follow his lead.
如果他愿意，如果他挺过来了，并向我们展示他想留在这里，那么我们将跟随他的脚步。

00:48:25.360 --> 00:48:41.349
And that is in many ways what the past 19 years of my life have been, but at the time, it felt horrible and alone and lost and scary and devastating.
在很多方面，我过去19年的生活就是这样，但在当时，这感觉很可怕、孤独、迷茫和令人崩溃。

00:48:41.349 --> 00:49:09.309
Once every two years, there's a conference of people of families, the Jacobson Syndrome community, thank God there's this one doctor in all of America who's the specialist and the expert in Jacobson syndrome, Dr. Paul Grossfeld, who's an amazing guy, who we raise money for on this podcast and we do a How Much Mother concert, fundraiser concert of the music of How Much Mother, we all rallied to raise money for this wonderful man who researches pediatric heart issues with a sort of subspecialty in Jacobson syndrome.
每两年，会有一个家庭会议，雅各布森综合征社区，感谢上帝，全美国只有一位医生是雅各布森综合征的专家，保罗·格罗斯菲尔德医生，他是一个了不起的人，我们在播客上为他筹款，我们举办了一场音乐会，筹集“How Much Mother”的音乐资金，我们都团结起来为这位研究儿科心脏问题并兼顾雅各布森综合征亚专科的了不起的人筹集资金。

00:49:10.309 --> 00:49:16.710
And we found him, thank goodness, In the course of those early couple months of Elliot's life, we found him.
谢天谢地，我们在埃利奥特出生后的头几个月里找到了他。

00:49:16.710 --> 00:49:18.389
This was a good use of the internet.
这是互联网的一次善用。

00:49:18.389 --> 00:49:25.789
Our dear friend, Robin Murgio, saw us breaking apart and said, I'm going to find who you should talk to.
我们亲爱的朋友罗宾·穆吉奥看到我们濒临崩溃，便说：“我要帮你们找到该咨询的人。”

00:49:25.789 --> 00:49:26.789
And she found him.
她真的找到了。

00:49:26.789 --> 00:49:28.429
And thank God he was only in San Diego.
谢天谢地，他就在圣地亚哥。

00:49:28.429 --> 00:49:32.469
We were able to drive down and spend several hours with him and have him take us through.
我们开车去见了他，和他待了几个小时，让他为我们详细讲解。

00:49:32.469 --> 00:49:38.429
He who had seen so many cases of Jacobson's syndrome, really the expert.
他见过许多雅各布森综合征的病例，确实是这方面的专家。

00:49:38.429 --> 00:49:48.690
He was able to paint us a much more refined, nuanced picture of what this was going to be, and I think sent us to this brighter path than we were in at the moment.
他为我们描绘了一幅更清晰、更细致的图景，让我们了解未来会怎样，我认为是他指引我们走上了一条比当时更光明的道路。

00:49:48.690 --> 00:49:54.630
I just described walking out of the bad newsroom, but all of this was, I probably visited Dr.
我刚才描述了走出那个噩耗房间的情景，但这一切……我大概是在筹备第三季时去圣地亚哥拜访了格罗斯菲尔德医生，因为在某个时间点，当埃利奥特在六周多后刚出院时，大概是五月中旬左右……

00:49:54.630 --> 00:50:10.309
Grossfeld in San Diego as we were launching season three because somewhere along the line, by the time Elliot was just coming home from the hospital, six and plus weeks later, whatever that would be mid-May or something, a mid...
我们五月大部分时间都在医院度过，大概六月初才回到家。

00:50:10.309 --> 00:50:14.449
We would have spent most of May in the hospital, then maybe early June we got home.
我们得开始筹备编剧室了。

00:50:14.449 --> 00:50:16.710
We're needing to start up the writer's room.
就是这样。

00:50:16.710 --> 00:50:17.550
That's it.
现在去写些有趣的段子，启动第三季吧，伙计。

00:50:17.550 --> 00:50:20.070
Now go be funny, launch season three, dude.
你就在这儿，疲惫的人没有休息的机会。

00:50:20.070 --> 00:50:23.389
Here you are, no rest for the weary.
但我发现，回到他身上——你知道，埃里克已经唠叨了一会儿了——谢天谢地，我有一个可以去思考别的事情的地方。

00:50:23.389 --> 00:50:36.199
But what I discovered, just to bring it back to him, you know, Eric's now been blabbering a while, is thank God I had this place to go to think about something else.
如果能重来，我可能会……我当时背负的东西，我无法过多地谈论它。

00:50:36.199 --> 00:50:48.059
And if I could go back, I would probably have, the thing I was carrying with me was not, I was not able to talk about it as much.
我做不到，我总是会淡化发生的事情。

00:50:48.059 --> 00:50:51.500
I was not able, I would always tone down what had happened.
我不想用真实的情况去给别人增加负担。

00:50:51.500 --> 00:50:55.219
Like I didn't wanna burden people with really what it was.
我感到羞愧或害怕。

00:50:55.219 --> 00:50:58.139
And I was ashamed or scared.
我不想强调他未来会有发育障碍、智力障碍和学习差异，因为我们生活在这个国家和文化中，从小伴随着那些带有污名化的词汇，以及谈论和看待智力障碍的方式长大，还有那个“R”开头的词，人们用诊断智力障碍的词汇来骂人愚蠢，以及我们贬低和轻视残疾人的方式。我是在80年代、90年代长大的，看看我们今天所处的环境。

00:50:58.139 --> 00:51:35.420
I didn't wanna play up the part where he was gonna have these developmental disabilities, his intellectual disabilities and learning differences because we live in this country and this culture and grew up with these stigmatized words and ways of talking about and looking at intellectual disability and the R word and the ways, you know, the people use words of diagnosis for intellectual disabilities as a way to call someone stupid and the ways we've denigrated and demeaned people with disabilities, I grew up in the 80s, you know, In the 90s, look where we are even today.
我认为我们在谈论残疾的方式上倒退了很多，尤其是在政治和社交媒体领域。

00:51:35.420 --> 00:51:40.739
I think we've taken a lot of steps back in the way that disability is being talked about, especially in the world of politics and social media.
我现在不想深入讨论这个，但我当时很想保护他。

00:51:40.739 --> 00:51:46.099
I won't get into that right now, but I was protective.
我会精挑细选，只告诉那些我真正信任的人完整的故事。

00:51:46.099 --> 00:51:50.139
I would cherry pick who I would really tell the full story to.
如果能重来，我会把一切都告诉所有人，不再试图隐瞒任何事情，也不再试图表现得比实际情况更好。

00:51:50.139 --> 00:51:58.139
And if I could go back, I would just tell everyone, everything all the time and not try to hide anything and not try to seem more okay than I was.
我不会再试图表现得比实际情况更好。

00:51:58.139 --> 00:52:03.579
But I slowly let people in more and more, but it took me a minute.
但我慢慢地越来越敞开心扉，但这花了我一点时间。

00:52:03.579 --> 00:52:15.500
And it was nice to go into how much mother and I did have people I could trust and tell the story to, but I also liked having this place to go and create another world and create something else to think about and exist in.
很高兴能深入谈谈我和母亲确实有一些我可以信任并倾诉的人，但我同时也喜欢拥有这样一个地方，去创造另一个世界，去创造一些别的东西来思考和存在。

00:52:15.500 --> 00:52:17.780
It kind of saved me, I think.
我想，这在某种程度上拯救了我。

00:52:17.780 --> 00:52:22.260
I do remember the equality to the way in the early years that you would talk about it.
我确实记得早些年你谈论这件事的方式，那种平淡感。

00:52:22.260 --> 00:52:35.820
That was, yeah, like not giving it the full weight of it to, Yeah, yeah, like almost like to not burden us with it.
是的，就像是没有赋予它全部的沉重感，是的，是的，几乎就像是不想让我们为此感到负担。

00:52:35.820 --> 00:52:40.420
And I know for me as your friend, like I wanted more of it.
我知道作为你的朋友，我当时想要了解更多。

00:52:40.420 --> 00:52:46.739
Like I wanted you to tell me, you know, when I think as the years went on, you did tell me more and more.
就像我希望你告诉我，你知道，随着时间的推移，你确实告诉了我越来越多。

00:52:46.739 --> 00:52:53.780
And I wanna share the story, which I shared with you a while back, but I remember I think it was Neil had a party he had all of us over.
我想分享一个故事，我很久以前和你分享过，但我记得好像是尼尔举办了一个派对，邀请了我们所有人。

00:52:53.780 --> 00:52:57.059
And it was like an afternoon like pool party.
那是一个下午的泳池派对。

00:52:57.059 --> 00:53:01.099
And I remember Rebecca was in the pool with Elliot and I think he, you know, he had floaties on.
我记得丽贝卡和埃利奥特在泳池里，我想他当时戴着浮力圈。

00:53:01.099 --> 00:53:04.579
He was like very little, but he was maybe two or three.
他当时很小，可能只有两三岁。

00:53:04.579 --> 00:53:05.739
Yeah.
是的。

00:53:06.900 --> 00:53:09.219
But I saw his scar on his chest.
但我看到了他胸口的伤疤。

00:53:09.219 --> 00:53:10.059
Yeah.
是的。

00:53:10.059 --> 00:53:14.699
And I was so startled by, I mean, it looks like a battle wound.
我当时非常震惊，我的意思是，那看起来像是一道战斗留下的伤痕。

00:53:14.699 --> 00:53:19.699
Like it looks like, it was dramatic, it was way more dramatic than I was almost picturing.
看起来非常戏剧性，比我脑海中想象的要戏剧性得多。

00:53:19.699 --> 00:53:27.980
And then to think of a three and a half pound baby with that kind of level of, you know, that kind of incision.
想到一个三磅半重的婴儿身上有那种程度的切口，你知道。

00:53:27.980 --> 00:53:51.280
And I think as the years have gone on, Craig, I have always, I don't know, it's even all these years later, 19 years later, 17, 19, 19, 19 years later, feeling like I'm still getting underneath this story of like what this was like for you in Rebecca, what this was like for Elliot, you know, what?
随着岁月流逝，克雷格，我一直，我不知道，即使是这么多年过去了，19年了，17年，19年，19年过去了，我感觉我仍然在深入了解这个故事，了解这对你和丽贝卡来说意味着什么，对埃利奥特来说意味着什么，你知道，是什么？

00:53:51.280 --> 00:54:01.119
And I really, I think like doing the benefit every year is like my way of just like loving you and loving your family.
我真的认为，每年举办慈善活动是我表达对你和你家人爱意的方式。

00:54:01.119 --> 00:54:06.019
And, you know, I remember, I hope it's okay to say this, and if it's not, we can cut it.
而且，你知道，我记得，希望说出来没关系，如果不合适，我们可以剪掉。

00:54:06.019 --> 00:54:18.000
But, you know, you told me that you had to talk to your therapist about just how many expectations were dashed of, you know, like, this kid's not gonna go to Wesleyan.
但是，你知道，你告诉我你不得不和治疗师谈论有多少期望落空了，你知道，比如，这孩子不会去卫斯理大学了。

00:54:18.000 --> 00:54:20.000
Like, this kid's not gonna follow your path.
这孩子不会走你的老路了。

00:54:20.000 --> 00:54:26.320
He's not gonna do the things that, you know, sports are gonna be, it's a different proposition.
他不会做那些事，你知道，体育运动将是另一回事了。

00:54:26.320 --> 00:54:27.320
That's different.
那是不同的。

00:54:27.320 --> 00:54:34.000
And but the joy, I think, and I don't know if we said this, but like Ellie, it's like a musical prodigy.
但我想，那种快乐，我不知道我们是否说过，但埃利奥特就像是一个音乐天才。

00:54:34.000 --> 00:54:37.079
Like he plays how he plays a ton of instruments, right?
就像他演奏很多乐器那样，对吧？

00:54:37.079 --> 00:54:39.559
He's got an incredible ear.
他有极好的乐感。

00:54:39.559 --> 00:54:41.920
He's got really good taste in music.
他的音乐品味非常好。

00:54:41.920 --> 00:54:50.599
And I think you told me that even when Jacobson's people have often will have like one quirky, amazing talent.
我想你曾告诉过我，即使是雅各布森（Jacobson）的人，通常也会有一种古怪而惊人的天赋。

00:54:50.599 --> 00:54:53.440
Yeah, kind of like that pokes through.
是的，就像那种显露出来的特质。

00:54:53.440 --> 00:55:02.986
And music is certainly, and you got, you know, like Marshall Eric said, like, you got to have the Thomas family players, you know, I get to be a, I'm in a family band.
音乐当然是其中之一，就像马歇尔·埃里克（Marshall Eric）说的，你得有托马斯家族乐手，你知道，我能成为其中的一员，我在一个家庭乐队里。

00:55:02.986 --> 00:55:09.961
I, you know, I play my, if I go back to that bad newsroom and to that moment and just say, just tie, I get time work back.
你知道，如果我能回到那个糟糕的新闻编辑室，回到那一刻，只要说，只要争取时间，我能穿越回去。

00:55:09.961 --> 00:55:10.875
And it's me now.
现在的我。

00:55:10.875 --> 00:55:16.249
And I walk in the room and I say, listen, real quick, before this fucking geneticist comes in.
我走进房间说，听着，快点，在那位该死的遗传学家进来之前。

00:55:16.249 --> 00:55:20.880
I just want to tell, Hey, Rebecca, Hey, Craig, I just want to tell you something.
我只想告诉你们，嘿，丽贝卡，嘿，克雷格，我只想告诉你们一件事。

