But also, basically, you're describing your how I met your mother, which is you wanted narrator, Craig.
但同时，你基本上是在描述你的《老爸老妈浪漫史》，也就是你想要的叙述者，克雷格。

00:56:46.070 --> 00:56:46.550
Yes.
是的。

00:56:46.550 --> 00:56:50.630
To be beamed into your ears.
被传送到你的耳朵里。

00:56:50.630 --> 00:57:07.309
Yes, it's like the 45 days, like if I could just go back and like just say this extra thing before I get to meet the mother, if I could just say this thing at this time, it's like that, it's the time traveler, it's wanting future media on that couch to be able to go back.
是的，就像那45天，如果我能回到过去，在见到老妈之前多说这一句话，如果我能在那个时候说出这件事，就像那样，这就是时间旅行者，是希望未来的自己在那张沙发上能够回到过去。

00:57:07.309 --> 00:57:09.750
And yeah, and it's...
是的，而且……

00:57:09.750 --> 00:57:25.219
But also, you know, when you think about what people love about how I met a mother, and we hear about it every week, we get letters, we get voice notes, The show was written by resilient people.
但同时，你知道，当你想到人们为什么喜欢《老爸老妈浪漫史》时，我们每周都能听到，我们收到信件，收到语音留言，这部剧是由坚韧的人创作的。

00:57:25.219 --> 00:57:51.900
The show was written, you know, you had actual skin in the game like when you were writing this stuff, even if it wasn't about your direct autobiographical experience, you were, you know, talk about, you know, change of expectation, having to find, having to pick yourself up from your lowest moment, everything getting contextualized in the end that was way, that organized itself way better than you could have ever.
这部剧的创作，你知道，当你写这些东西时，你确实投入了真情实感，即使它不是关于你直接的自传经历，你也在谈论，你知道，期望的改变，必须找到出路，必须从最低谷中振作起来，最终一切都变得有意义，这种自我组织的方式比你想象的要好得多。

00:57:51.900 --> 00:57:58.500
I remember, you know, we have a great love for my friend Lucy Walker's film, The Crash Reel, which is just, if you haven't seen this movie.
我记得，你知道，我们非常喜欢我朋友露西·沃克（Lucy Walker）的电影《碰撞》（The Crash Reel），如果你还没看过这部电影的话。

00:57:58.500 --> 00:58:00.139
It's an amazing documentary.
这是一部了不起的纪录片。

00:58:00.139 --> 00:58:00.980
By the way, John's got this documentary.
顺便说一下，约翰有这部纪录片。

00:58:00.980 --> 00:58:03.179
And influence on my novel, right?
对我小说有影响，对吧？

00:58:03.179 --> 00:58:15.099
Yes, yes, yes, seeing that movie and this family and the character with the character, the actual human being and the documentary with Down Syndrome and how his family is with him, influenced my novel a bunch.
是的，是的，是的，看到那部电影，看到这个家庭，看到那个角色，那个真实的人，那个患有唐氏综合症的纪录片主角，以及他的家人如何对待他，对我小说影响很大。

00:58:15.099 --> 00:58:22.699
So this movie is about a guy who is a world-class snowboarder, and he was on his way to the Olympics.
所以这部电影讲的是一个世界级的单板滑雪运动员，他当时正准备参加奥运会。

00:58:22.699 --> 00:58:25.739
He was like Sean White's main competitor, American competitor.
他就像肖恩·怀特（Shaun White）的主要竞争对手，美国选手。

00:58:25.739 --> 00:58:30.940
And he took a terrible, terrible fall in Park City on a practice run, had a traumatic brain injury.
他在帕克城的练习赛中摔得很惨，遭受了严重的脑损伤。

00:58:30.940 --> 00:58:36.340
And it's really about his family adapting to his injury, but his younger brother has Down Syndrome.
这实际上是关于他的家人如何适应他的伤情，但他有个患有唐氏综合症的弟弟。

00:58:36.340 --> 00:58:38.659
And he's just this incredible character.
他真是个不可思议的角色。

00:58:38.659 --> 00:58:41.780
And the parents are unbelievable in that film.
那部电影里的父母简直令人难以置信。

00:58:41.780 --> 00:58:45.980
just like really loving, beautiful, present people.
他们真的是非常有爱心、美好、活在当下的人。

00:58:45.980 --> 00:58:59.579
And I remember the father has a thing we said, you know, if people, if you told me like, I could have another son without Down Syndrome or not have this kid, I would say never in a million years would I trade this kid.
我记得那位父亲说过一句话，你知道，如果有人告诉我，我可以选择要一个没有唐氏综合症的儿子，或者不要这个孩子，我会说，一百万年我也不会换掉这个孩子。

00:58:59.579 --> 00:59:01.340
Like he is perfect to me.
对我来说，他是完美的。

00:59:01.340 --> 00:59:02.980
He's perfect for our family.
他对于我们的家庭来说是完美的。

00:59:02.980 --> 00:59:06.539
And I just, I remember that as it was- You turn me on to that movie.
我记得当时——是你向我推荐了那部电影。

00:59:06.539 --> 00:59:08.460
I remember in that movie, oh my God.
我记得在那部电影里，天哪。

00:59:08.460 --> 00:59:11.179
I sobbed crying at the end of that movie.
电影结尾我哭得稀里哗啦。

00:59:11.179 --> 00:59:12.820
It meant a lot to me.
这对我意义重大。

00:59:12.820 --> 00:59:25.500
I was able to share a lot of this with you and you were very present with me at the time and in the years after I feel like you were one of the few people that I could talk to deeply about it.
我能和你分享很多这些事情，当时你一直陪伴着我，在随后的几年里，我觉得你是少数几个我能深入谈论这些事的人之一。

00:59:25.500 --> 00:59:27.699
And that remained true the whole run of the show.
这一点在整个剧集制作期间一直没变。

00:59:27.699 --> 00:59:28.820
You've been so supportive.
你一直非常支持我。

00:59:28.820 --> 00:59:32.380
You've been so amazing with the fundraising for Dr. Grossfeld.
你在为格罗斯菲尔德医生（Dr. Grossfeld）筹款方面做得太棒了。

00:59:32.380 --> 00:59:34.300
You've helped in so many ways over the years.
这些年来，你在很多方面都帮了我。

00:59:34.300 --> 00:59:37.539
You helped me with my book, which was inspired by Elliot.
你帮我完成了我的书，那本书的灵感来源于埃利奥特（Elliot）。

00:59:37.539 --> 00:59:40.791
You were the audio book narrator of my book, inspire belly.
你还是我那本《Inspire Belly》有声书的朗读者。

00:59:40.791 --> 00:59:45.586
You've done so much to support me on this journey that I just, it's nice, I don't know.
你在这一路上给了我太多的支持，我只是……这感觉很好，我不知道该怎么说。

00:59:45.586 --> 00:59:48.231
It's nice to have the opportunity to say thanks.
有机会说声谢谢真好。

00:59:48.231 --> 00:59:51.538
I hope I do often enough because I really feel that support.
我希望我表达得足够频繁，因为我真的感受到了那份支持。

00:59:51.538 --> 00:59:58.923
I think that I got the sense that you probably didn't have a lot of people to talk to about it and sometimes people get scared to ask.
我想我当时感觉到，你可能没有太多人可以倾诉，有时人们会害怕询问。

00:59:58.923 --> 01:00:03.718
I think people didn't know how to talk about it and I didn't know how to talk about it.
我觉得人们不知道该怎么谈论这件事，我也不知道该怎么谈论。

01:00:03.718 --> 01:00:05.316
I know everyone was learning.
我知道每个人都在学习。

01:00:05.316 --> 01:00:06.860
It's not It's not just them.
不仅仅是他们。

01:00:06.860 --> 01:00:07.860
It's me too.
我也是。

01:00:07.860 --> 01:00:08.860
Yeah.
是啊。

01:00:08.860 --> 01:00:09.860
Yeah.
是啊。

01:00:09.860 --> 01:00:16.039
And I remember there was a point where I made it a point to check in with you about like specifically you would.
我记得有段时间，我特意找你沟通，特别是你……

01:00:16.039 --> 01:00:17.039
You would.
你会的。

01:00:17.039 --> 01:00:18.039
Yes.
是的。

01:00:18.039 --> 01:00:19.039
You would do that.
你会那样做。

01:00:19.039 --> 01:00:20.039
Rob Greenberg would do that.
罗伯·格林伯格（Rob Greenberg）也会那样做。

01:00:20.039 --> 01:00:21.039
And I don't just sit here singling.
我不是在这里专门点名。

01:00:21.039 --> 01:00:22.039
I'll be the people that would or wouldn't do that.
我会说出那些会或不会这样做的人。

01:00:22.039 --> 01:00:27.440
But like there were just a few people who were a little more fearless about opening it up.
但确实有那么几个人，在敞开心扉这件事上更无畏一些。

01:00:27.440 --> 01:00:28.440
Not just how's it going?
不仅仅是问“最近怎么样？”

01:00:28.440 --> 01:00:32.079
And I think they just want me to say it's going well.
我想他们只是想让我说“一切都好”。

01:00:32.079 --> 01:00:33.079
Yeah.
是啊。

01:00:33.079 --> 01:00:34.599
and I give the real answer.
而我给出了真实的答案。

01:00:35.719 --> 01:00:39.639
And you know, it's scary when things happen.
你知道，当事情发生时，这很可怕。

01:00:39.639 --> 01:00:51.599
It's almost like this fear that like hard things are contagious or like we, you know, there's a real thing of like, you know, keep it nice, like keep it like, I don't want that to invade the space.
这就像有一种恐惧，觉得困难的事情会传染，或者我们有一种心态，你知道，保持体面，保持……我不想让这些事侵入我们的空间。

01:00:51.599 --> 01:01:05.679
Yeah, but the truth is like when we don't talk about those things, they have so much more power and they're so much scarier than they would otherwise be, you know, like creating like a kind of freedom of Information Act around struggles is a really good thing.
是啊，但事实是，当我们不谈论这些事情时，它们反而拥有了更大的力量，比原本更可怕。你知道，围绕这些挣扎建立一种“信息自由法”其实是一件好事。

01:01:05.679 --> 01:01:15.159
Freedom of Information Act, I wish that is a wonderful way to say it, I wish that that existed and that you were legally required to unburden yourself and trust your everyone and talk about stuff.
“信息自由法”，我希望……这真是一个绝妙的说法，我希望它真的存在，而且每个人在法律上都被要求卸下负担，信任彼此，谈论这些事情。

01:01:15.159 --> 01:01:22.320
And that's the culture we lived in, it's not really, you kind of get there over many years and a lot of work and a lot of therapy, I think.
但我们生活的文化并非如此，你得经过多年的努力和大量的心理治疗才能达到那种境界，我想。

01:01:22.320 --> 01:01:33.159
But I remember, I mean, I remember being scared, I was 31 years old, running a TV show, my first TV show I was running or creating, really my first sitcom writing staff.
但我记得，我是说，我记得当时很害怕，我才31岁，在运营一部电视剧，这是我运营或创作的第一部电视剧，也是我第一个情景喜剧编剧团队。

01:01:33.159 --> 01:01:35.639
I was really on and we were the boss.
我当时真的……我们是老板。

01:01:35.639 --> 01:01:37.440
And I remember being scared of so many things.
我记得我害怕很多事情。

01:01:37.440 --> 01:01:41.960
Among them, I didn't want to freak everyone out that I couldn't do the job anymore.
其中之一是，我不想让大家惊慌，觉得我无法再胜任这份工作了。

01:01:41.960 --> 01:01:43.280
Now go be funny.
“现在去搞笑吧。”

01:01:43.280 --> 01:01:48.519
Like I didn't want like the studio and the network to think like Craig's having a nervous breakdown.
我不想让制片厂和电视网觉得克雷格（Craig）要精神崩溃了。

01:01:48.519 --> 01:01:50.039
His life's falling apart.
他的生活正在分崩离析。

01:01:50.039 --> 01:01:51.679
Craig can't be funny anymore.
克雷格再也写不出好笑的东西了。

01:01:51.679 --> 01:01:54.559
I'd like up in my own, no one would have thought that.
我当时想……其实没人会那么想。

01:01:54.559 --> 01:01:56.639
I don't think or if they did, they would have had sympathy.
我不这么认为，或者如果他们真的这么想，他们也会表示同情。

01:01:56.639 --> 01:01:58.599
But in your head, you build up these fears.
但在你脑海里，你会建立起这些恐惧。

01:01:58.599 --> 01:02:00.599
This people will look at me differently.
人们会用不同的眼光看我。

01:02:00.599 --> 01:02:02.079
people will look at my kid differently.
人们会用不同的眼光看我的孩子。

01:02:02.079 --> 01:02:17.280
There will be a stigma on my kid and on me and on my family, and you have so many fears, and you're so afraid of being othered and not accepted that you, I think, circle the wagons more than you need to, and you don't let as many people in as you should.
我的孩子、我和我的家人会背上污名，你有太多的恐惧，你非常害怕被边缘化、不被接受，以至于我认为你比实际需要地更加自我封闭，没有让足够多的人走进你的生活。

01:02:17.280 --> 01:02:31.239
And if I had a piece of advice on this, I would say, open up as soon as you can and as wide as you can when something like this happens to you and to your kid, led people in ask for help, be transparent.
如果我能给出一项建议，我会说，当这种事情发生在你和你的孩子身上时，尽可能早、尽可能彻底地敞开心扉，让人们进来，寻求帮助，保持透明。

01:02:31.239 --> 01:02:33.039
I don't think I was fully at first.
我觉得我起初并没有完全做到。

01:02:33.039 --> 01:02:35.719
I think also though, find the community.
不过我也认为，要找到那个群体。

01:02:35.719 --> 01:02:37.840
Like once you found the other families.
就像你找到其他家庭之后那样。

01:02:37.840 --> 01:02:38.840
Yeah, that really helped.
是啊，那真的很有帮助。

01:02:38.840 --> 01:02:40.159
Something shifted for you guys.
你们的情况发生了转变。

01:02:40.159 --> 01:02:41.480
Yeah, we found the community.
是啊，我们找到了那个群体。

01:02:41.480 --> 01:02:43.800
We started going to the conferences.
我们开始参加各种会议。

01:02:43.800 --> 01:02:49.320
Dr. Grossfeld became a dear friend and the person we could call in an emergency.
格罗斯菲尔德医生（Dr. Grossfeld）成了我们亲密的朋友，也是我们在紧急情况下可以求助的人。

01:02:49.320 --> 01:02:57.559
And the Hymium community and everyone that worked on Hymium with me was so there for me and so loving.
《老爸老妈浪漫史》（HIMYM）的团队以及所有和我一起工作的人都非常支持我，非常关爱我。

01:02:57.559 --> 01:03:02.000
And I got to talk about it sometimes, and I also got to escape it sometimes.
我有机会谈论这件事，有时也有机会逃避它。

01:03:02.000 --> 01:03:06.440
And I got to go say, what's happening for Ted in season three?
我可以去问，泰德（Ted）在第三季会发生什么？

01:03:06.440 --> 01:03:08.360
What's happening for Marshall in season three?
马歇尔（Marshall）在第三季会发生什么？

01:03:08.360 --> 01:03:09.760
Where's Robinette?
罗宾（Robin）去哪了？

01:03:09.760 --> 01:03:11.760
But what are we gonna make up?
我们要编造什么？

01:03:11.760 --> 01:03:30.280
What funny thing can we make up to escape into together and have everybody yes end that and create this other world to walk into for a little while was incredibly healing and the how much mother of fans and the audience that watched the show and kept it alive.
我们可以一起编造什么有趣的事情来逃避现实，让每个人都用“是的，而且……”（yes, and）来接话，创造出这个可以暂时进入的另一个世界，这具有难以置信的治愈作用。而《老爸老妈浪漫史》的粉丝以及观看这部剧并让它延续下去的观众……

01:03:30.280 --> 01:03:37.199
Just one little side benefit of that is that it got me through the hardest time in my life.
这带来的一个小小的额外好处是，它帮我度过了人生中最艰难的时期。

01:03:38.199 --> 01:03:45.400
And it cost a lot of money to have a kid with lifelong disabilities.
养育一个有终身残疾的孩子需要花很多钱。

01:03:45.400 --> 01:03:49.159
It really, it doesn't hurt to have some money.
真的，有些钱在手里总没坏处。

01:03:49.159 --> 01:03:58.239
And I felt this huge pressure that the show had the last and be a hit and work, because this would be my chance to pay for Elliot's life forever.
我感到一种巨大的压力，这部剧必须长久，必须成功，必须火起来，因为这是我永远支付埃利奥特（Elliot）生活费的机会。

01:03:58.239 --> 01:04:06.000
And I felt that very acutely in the moment, because I think other families that have gone through this start getting sent those bills.
当时我对此感受非常强烈，因为我想其他经历过这些的家庭开始收到那些账单时也是一样。

01:04:06.000 --> 01:04:15.559
And you see what the therapies cost, and you see what the medical bills are, and you see what the future is, and what education is, and you really go, dear God and heaven, how will we do this?
你看到了治疗的费用，看到了医疗账单，看到了未来，看到了教育支出，你真的会想，天哪，我们该怎么负担这些？

01:04:15.559 --> 01:04:22.559
And so I will say that one other thing that came out of that was this fierce drive to make this show succeed.
所以我得说，由此产生的另一件事就是让这部剧成功的强烈动力。

01:04:22.559 --> 01:04:30.199
I was just sent on a crazy mission of, I have, this has to work, this has to work.
我当时就像被派去执行一个疯狂的任务，心想：这必须成功，这必须成功。

01:04:30.199 --> 01:04:40.719
So I think I was a bit manic, after I was manic to begin with, I wanted the show to be ahead, I wanted the show to work to begin with, but this was like, I developed a kind of like, you were a relaxed person, the first two seasons.
所以我认为我当时有点狂躁，虽然我本来就有点狂躁，我本来就希望这部剧能火，希望它能成功，但这就像是……你是一个很放松的人，前两季的时候。

01:04:40.719 --> 01:04:54.400
You were here to tell me, I was not relaxed to begin with, but I know I definitely came in with this kind of crazed edge of like, this is it, we're gonna make this fucking work.
你在这里告诉我，我起初并不放松，但我知道我确实带着一种疯狂的边缘感进来的，就像是：就是现在，我们要让这该死的东西成功。

01:04:54.960 --> 01:05:00.559
And I don't know if that was good or bad or healthy or unhealthy, but it's just what it was.
我不知道这到底是好是坏，是健康还是不健康，但事实就是这样。

01:05:00.559 --> 01:05:04.280
And I came in, I wanted season three to be fucking great.
我进来时，就想让第三季变得非常棒。

01:05:04.280 --> 01:05:07.880
And when that writer's gold strike hit, I was terrified.
当编剧大罢工来袭时，我吓坏了。

01:05:07.880 --> 01:05:11.280
I couldn't believe we were stopping and ceasing momentum.
我无法相信我们竟然要停下来，中断势头。

01:05:11.280 --> 01:05:15.619
And when we came out of that writer's gold strike, we had to do nine episodes in about 11 weeks.
当编剧大罢工结束后，我们必须在大约11周内完成9集。

01:05:15.619 --> 01:05:17.960
And I was like, bring it on, man.
我当时想，来吧，伙计们。

01:05:17.960 --> 01:05:23.960
And I think everyone else around me knew that and wanted it to work for me too.
我想我周围的每个人都知道这一点，也希望它能为我成功。

01:05:23.960 --> 01:05:29.039
And I think I felt like everyone was really on mission and wanted it to work.
我想我感觉到每个人都真的在执行任务，并且希望它能成功。

01:05:29.039 --> 01:05:33.719
I mean, it's amazing what happened between legend and dairy.
我是说，在“Legend”和“Dary”之间发生的事情太神奇了。

01:05:33.719 --> 01:05:41.400
The pause between legend and dairy was in and of itself legendary and epic and just unbelievable.
“Legend”和“Dary”之间的停顿本身就是传奇、史诗般的，简直令人难以置信。

01:05:41.400 --> 01:05:49.404
Look at what happened between the two halves that word, Josh, and how much our lives changed before we got to that dairy.
看看那个词的两部分之间发生了什么，乔什（Josh），在我们到达那个“Dary”之前，我们的生活发生了多大的变化。

01:05:49.404 --> 01:05:53.307
It's extraordinary, and I think it was worth talking about.
这太不寻常了，我认为这值得一谈。

01:05:53.307 --> 01:06:06.340
